  "prediction": "**Oldest Cold Case Ever Brought to Court: Man Released After 58 Years**\n\nIn a historic turn of events, Jack McCullough, a 76-year-old man from Washington state, has been released from prison after serving 58 years for the murder of 7-year-old Maria Ridulph in 1957. The case, which was one of the oldest unsolved crimes in the United States, has been reopened and re-examined, leading to a judge's decision to grant McCullough a new trial and release him from prison.\n\nMaria Ridulph was abducted while playing in the snow with a friend in Sycamore, Illinois, on December 3, 1957. Her body was found five months later, and the case went cold for decades. In 2012, McCullough was convicted of the crime and sentenced to life in prison, but a new investigation led by DeKalb County State's Attorney Richard Schmack has raised questions about the evidence used to convict him.\n\nSchmack's six-month review of the case found \"clear and convincing evidence\" that McCullough was wrongly convicted. He discovered new phone records that supported McCullough's alibi, which was that he was in Rockford, Illinois, at the time of the abduction, attempting to enlist in the U.S. Air Force. Schmack also reviewed police reports and hundreds of other documents that had been improperly barred at trial, which he said contained \"a wealth of information\" pointing to McCullough's innocence.\n\nJudge William P. Brady, who presided over the hearing, pointed to several factors in his decision to release McCullough, including the new evidence and the fact that McCullough's conviction was based on false testimony and improper rulings. McCullough's family members were overjoyed by the decision, while Maria's family remains convinced of his guilt and has sought the appointment of a special prosecutor to keep him behind bars.\n\nThe case has sparked a renewed debate about the reliability of eyewitness testimony and the importance of re-examining old cases. It also highlights the challenges of prosecuting cold cases, where evidence is often lost or destroyed, and memories fade with time.\n\nIn a statement, Schmack said, \"I am confident that we have done the right thing in releasing Mr. McCullough. The evidence is clear, and it is time to give him the justice he deserves.\"\n\nMcCullough's release is a significant development in one of the most infamous cold cases in American",
